If Collection has a method, does List also have it?

Yes.

Because:

Collection
     ↑
    List

List extends Collection.

Therefore, whatever methods are available in Collection are also available through List.

For example, Collection has:

add()
remove()
size()
contains()
isEmpty()

So a List also has them:

List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

list.add(10);
list.remove(10);
list.size();
list.contains(10);
list.isEmpty();

You don't get a completely different version of add() just because you're using List.

Think inheritance:
Collection
   |
   |  add()
   |  remove()
   |  size()
   |  contains()
   ↓
List
   |
   |  gets all of the above
   |
   |  + get()
   |  + set()
   |  + add(index, element)
   |  + indexOf()
   ↓
ArrayList

So List inherits the Collection methods and adds its own methods.

2. Does List have additional features compared to Collection?

Yes. This is the key difference.

Collection is general.

It doesn't assume that elements have positions/indexes.

For example, a Set doesn't have:

0th element
1st element
2nd element

So Collection cannot provide:

get(2);

because that doesn't make sense for every Collection.

But List specifically represents an ordered, positional collection.

Therefore, List can provide:

get(2);
set(2, 100);
add(2, 50);
remove(2);
indexOf(50);

So:

Collection
    ↓
General operations
    ↓
add()
remove()
size()
contains()

List
    ↓
Everything from Collection
    +
List-specific operations
    ↓
get(index)
set(index, element)
add(index, element)
indexOf()

That's why List is more specific than Collection.

3. Now the most important question: What does "ArrayList provides concrete implementation" mean?

This is where I think your confusion is happening.

When we say:

"ArrayList provides a concrete implementation of List."

we mean:

List only defines what operations should be available. ArrayList defines how those operations actually work internally.

This is extremely important.

Think about List

Suppose List says:

list.add(10);

The interface essentially says:

"Any class implementing me must provide an add() operation."

But List doesn't say:

"Store 10 inside an array at position 3."

It doesn't care how.

ArrayList makes that decision

ArrayList says:

"I will implement List using a dynamically resizable array internally."

Conceptually:

List
 │
 │ says WHAT operations must exist
 ↓
ArrayList
 │
 │ decides HOW to perform them
 ↓
Internal array

For example:

List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

list.add(10);
list.add(20);
list.add(30);

Internally, conceptually, ArrayList maintains something like:

Internal array:

[10] [20] [30] [ ] [ ] [ ]

You don't see that array directly.

ArrayList manages it for you.

4. So does ArrayList have its own methods?

Yes, but be careful with what you mean.

ArrayList implements the methods required by List, such as:

add()
remove()
get()
set()
size()

The important point is that the implementation is inside ArrayList.

For example:

List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

When you write:

list.add(10);

the add() method that actually executes is the implementation provided by ArrayList.

Conceptually:

You write:

list.add(10)
      ↓
List says:
"add() should exist"
      ↓
Actual object is ArrayList
      ↓
ArrayList's implementation of add()
      ↓
stores 10 in its internal array
